     "text": "Willis LLC Clinic\n\nDear Colleague,\n\nI saw Alec Alexander in consultation on May 01, 2024. The patient is a 65-year-old male with a history of Atrial fibrillation / flutter diagnosed on March 03, 2000. The echocardiogram results from the most recent evaluation are normal, and the patient has a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 3. The patient has been in a controlled rhythm for approximately 24.9 years now. His current medications include rivaroxaban 10 mg.\n\nDuring this consultation, the patient’s blood pressure was recorded at 134/81 mmHg, and his weight was 240 pounds. Upon examination, he exhibited no significant abnormalities. His heart rhythm was regular, and there were no signs of structural heart disease. The patient reports adherence to his anticoagulation therapy with no recent episodes of arrhythmia.\n\nRegarding lifestyle factors, Mr. Alexander maintains an active routine, running 3-4 times per week. This level of physical activity is commendable and likely contributes to his overall cardiovascular health. On the social history side, Mr. Alexander consumes approximately 38 alcoholic drinks per week. While this is above the recommended limits, he does not report any associated complications or excessive use.\n\n**Impression and Recommendations:**\n\nAlec Alexander presents with a well-controlled history of Atrial fibrillation / flutter. His CHA2DS2-VASc score of 3 indicates a moderate risk for stroke, which is appropriately managed with rivaroxaban. The patient’s echocardiographic findings are normal, and there is no evidence of structural heart disease. His current regimen with rivaroxaban 10 mg appears effective in maintaining rhythm control.\n\nTo continue optimizing his cardiovascular health, I recommend Mr. Alexander maintain his current medication regimen and physical activity levels. If he chooses to reduce his alcohol consumption, it would be prudent to do so gradually under medical supervision. Routine follow-up with his primary care provider and periodic echocardiographic assessments are advised to monitor his condition.\n\nThank you for referring Mr. Alexander for this consultation. I appreciate your collaboration in managing his care.\n\nSincerely,\n\nDr. Gary Bauer \nCardiologist \nWillis LLC Clinic\n"
